narrative:
## The Type IS the Policy

Every password in your system must be at least 8 characters. Make that a type invariant and every function that works with passwords is guaranteed to receive a valid one:

```xiom
type Password = {
  value: Str;
  invariant: string.str_len(value) >= 8;
}

fn Password.strength(self: &Self) -> Int {
  let score = 0;
  if string.str_len(self.value) >= 12 { score = score + 1; }
  if self.value.has_uppercase() { score = score + 1; }
  if self.value.has_lowercase() { score = score + 1; }
  if self.value.has_digit() { score = score + 1; }
  if self.value.has_special() { score = score + 1; }
  return score;
}
```

`strength` never checks the minimum length — the invariant already guarantees it. The method can focus purely on scoring character diversity:

```xiom
fn Password.mask(self: &Self) -> Str {
  let masked = "";
  var i = 0;
  while i < string.str_len(self.value) {
    masked = masked + "*";
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return masked;
}
```

`mask` produces a string of asterisks equal to the password length. No password content is ever leaked — and the invariant guarantees we always have at least 8 asterisks. `mask` works on any valid Password without defensive checks.

